WEBVTT

00:00:00.103 --> 00:00:02.603
This is your first more independent table-writing

00:00:02.629 --> 00:00:06.589
exercise Do not start from a blank

00:00:06.602 --> 00:00:09.986
screen mentally Use the comments and earlier

00:00:09.999 --> 00:00:14.135
examples as a pattern Decide what one

00:00:14.161 --> 00:00:17.335
row in your small table represents Choose

00:00:17.361 --> 00:00:19.783
a primary key identify which columns are

00:00:19.796 --> 00:00:21.809
required and add a simple CHECK or

00:00:21.822 --> 00:00:25.295
other rule where it makes sense Then

00:00:25.322 --> 00:00:27.085
insert one valid row and run a

00:00:27.111 --> 00:00:31.076
SELECT to inspect it Use the browser

00:00:31.102 --> 00:00:32.812
editor and run your code in small

00:00:32.839 --> 00:00:37.424
steps if needed If PostgreSQL reports an

00:00:37.450 --> 00:00:39.569
error read it carefully and fix one

00:00:39.595 --> 00:00:43.178
issue at a time The slide includes

00:00:43.204 --> 00:00:45.310
one possible answer but try your own

00:00:45.336 --> 00:00:48.483
version first The purpose is to practice

00:00:48.510 --> 00:00:51.996
translating a small design into executable
PostgreSQL

00:00:52.260 --> 00:00:54.220
not to reproduce one exact script
